<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wallace Carothers</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1896">1896</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Wallace Hume Carothers</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wallace Hume Carothers was an American chemist, born in Burlington, Iowa, in 1896, who led organic chemistry at DuPont and is credited with the invention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nylon">nylon</a>. After a PhD at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> in 1924 and teaching posts that included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>, he joined DuPont's new fundamental research programme at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilmington-delaware">Wilmington, Delaware</a>, where he chose polymer research. His group helped lay the groundwork for neoprene, and in 1935 Gerard Berchet, under his direction, produced polyamide 6-6, the substance later known as nylon. Troubled by depression since his youth, he took his own life in 1937, sixteen months before the discovery of nylon was announced.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wallace-carothers.jpg" alt="Wallace Carothers" width="300" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown photographer · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wallace_Carothers,_in_the_lab.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Carothers was the oldest of four children of Ira and Mary Evalina Carothers. As a youth he was fascinated by tools and mechanical devices. Under pressure from his father he completed an accountancy course in Des Moines in 1915, then entered Tarkio College in Missouri, where he switched from English to chemistry under Arthur Pardee and graduated in 1920. He took a master's degree at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> in 1921 under Carl Marvel, and his doctorate under Roger Adams in 1924, and taught at Illinois and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harvard-university">Harvard University</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1927 DuPont decided to fund fundamental research, and Carothers hesitated, writing that he suffered from neurotic spells of diminished capacity. A DuPont executive persuaded him; his salary rose from $267 a month at Harvard to $500. He began work at the Experimental Station near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilmington-delaware">Wilmington, Delaware</a>, on 6 February 1928 and chose polymer research. In April 1930 Arnold M. Collins of his staff isolated chloroprene, which polymerised into a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>-like solid, the first synthetic rubber, named neoprene. The same year Julian W. Hill produced a polyester that could be drawn into fibres, but it softened in hot water, and Carothers put polymer research aside for several years.</p><p>In 1934 the group substituted diamines for glycols to make polyamides, which were far more stable. On 28 February 1935 Gerard Berchet, under Carothers's direction, produced a half-ounce of polyamide 6-6 from hexamethylenediamine and adipic acid. Its high melting point made it hard to work with, but Elmer K. Bolton chose it for commercial development, and George Graves eventually replaced Carothers as leader of the project.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Carothers suffered from depression through much of his life. In 1934 he disappeared and was found at a psychiatric clinic near Baltimore. He married Helen Sweetman, a DuPont chemist, on 21 February 1936, and on 30 April 1936 he became the first industrial organic chemist elected to the National Academy of Sciences. His sister Isobel died in January 1937, and he died in April 1937. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nylon">Nylon</a> was announced to the public on 27 October 1938. He was inducted into the Alpha Chi Sigma Hall of Fame in 1982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wallace Carothers” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>DuPont received the patent for nylon, invented by a team led by <b>Wallace Carothers</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-16">16 February in history</a></li></ul></section>
